  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which of the following is a factor of ${u}^{2}-4u+40?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we let $f(u)={u}^{2}-4u+40.$\n\nNext, we solve $f(u)=0$ by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(u) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{2}-4u+40 & =0 \\\\ ({u}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot u+{2}^{2})-{2}^{2}+40 & =0 \\\\ (u-2)^{2}-4+40 & =0 \\\\ (u-2)^{2}+36 & =0 \\\\ (u-2)^{2} & =-36 \\\\ (u-2)^{2} & =36{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\ (u-2)^{2} & =(6\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ u-2 & =\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ u & =2\\pm 6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our quadratic equation has roots ${u}_{1}=2+6\\text{i}$ and ${u}_{2}=2-6\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=1,$ the expression ${u}^{2}-4u+40$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-4u+40 & =a(u-{u}_{1})(u-{u}_{2}) \\\\  & =(u-(2+6\\text{i}))(u-(2-6\\text{i})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the given options, only $u-(2-6\\text{i})$ is a factor of ${u}^{2}-4u+40.$",
